# Self-signed HTTPS for LAN-only access — no public domain or Let's Encrypt needed.
#
# `tls internal` tells Caddy to mint certs from its own local root CA (generated
# on first run and persisted in the caddy-data volume) instead of requesting one
# from Let's Encrypt. This works for LAN IPs and local hostnames, which public
# CAs can't issue certs for anyway.
#
# Browsers will still show a certificate warning until Caddy's root CA is
# installed in the trust store of each device that connects — see the README
# section "HTTPS on your local network (self-signed, no domain)".
#
# The address below is resolved from the CADDY_HOST env var at load time (Caddy
# reads {$VAR} placeholders from the container's environment) — the committed
# Caddyfile already looks exactly like this, so you never need to edit it or
# commit your own address. Just set CADDY_HOST in .env:
#
#   CADDY_HOST=192.168.1.50, quillit.local
#
# Use a STABLE address here, not whatever DHCP happens to hand out today —
# otherwise the cert (and CORS_ORIGIN) breaks the next time the lease renews.
# Two ways to get one (see the README section "Stable LAN address"):
#   - Reserve the current LAN IP for this machine's MAC in your router's DHCP
#     settings, or
#   - Use the <hostname>.local mDNS alias that setup.sh's avahi-daemon step
#     advertises automatically (find it with: hostname).
# You can list both, comma-separated, so either one works if the other fails.
#
# This file is kept as a reference / reset point — restore it over Caddyfile
# if you ever need to start over: cp Caddyfile.internal.example Caddyfile
{$CADDY_HOST} {
    tls internal
    reverse_proxy ui:80
}
